==============================================================================
S5  Claim (iii): Perron-Frobenius and the sink/source shift
==============================================================================
Every square 0/1 matrix M is nonnegative, so rho(M) is an eigenvalue (Perron-Frobenius); Re(lambda)<0 for all lambda is impossible.
Sweep: matrices whose eigenvalues all have Re<0: ONE 0, TWO 0, ORI 0 (of 64 each); matrices with max Re >= 0: 64, 64, 64.
Sink/source shift: appending a sink (zero row) or a source (zero column) multiplies the characteristic polynomial by x.
  charpoly(M) == x^k charpoly(A) (k = number of appended polar vertices): 32 of 32 cases
  Negative real eigenvalues do occur (from cyclic tournament blocks), but never for every eigenvalue: sweep count has_negative_real_eig ONE 48 TWO 64 ORI 62.
Integer dichotomy: a nonnegative integer matrix has rho = 0 (nilpotent) or rho >= 1, never 0 < rho < 1 (tr M^k is a nonnegative integer; a closed walk gives tr M^k >= 1 for infinitely many k, and tr M^k <= N rho^k).
  sweep: 174 of 192 matrices have rho > 0; minimum nonzero rho = 1.0000; maximum rho = 5.4592 (TWO, m=n=4)
  Discrete-time contraction x -> M x needs rho(M) < 1, not Re(lambda) < 0; for a 0/1 matrix that forces nilpotency, i.e. an acyclic digraph.

